WEBVTT

NOTE How evidence becomes a forecast

1
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.241
A forty-year-old woman gets a routine breast cancer screening.

2
00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.067
Her test result is positive.

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:16.541
In a 1982 study, ninety-five out of one hundred doctors said her chance of cancer

4
00:00:16.541 --> 00:00:19.433
was between seventy and eighty percent.

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.299
The correct answer was about eight percent.

6
00:00:23.299 --> 00:00:27.567
Most doctors gave an answer almost ten times too high.

7
00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:31.495
How can the same test lead to such different numbers?

8
00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:35.533
Where does a probability come from, and how can we test it?

9
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:42.701
A probability sums up evidence. It is not a hidden fact waiting inside the world.

10
00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:48.133
We must choose the evidence, judge its limits, and test the result.

11
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:55.975
We will make five stops: the group being counted, the update after new evidence, the

12
00:00:55.975 --> 00:01:01.233
width of the answer, the kind of future claim, and the score.

13
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:03.910
First ask what the number counts.

14
00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.567
That question will show where the doctors went wrong.

15
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:11.581
A reference class is the group used for a count.

16
00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:17.641
Philosopher Alan Hájek belongs to several groups: men, non-smokers, and philosophy

17
00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:22.667
professors. Each group gives him different odds of living to eighty.

18
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:29.543
In 1814, Pierre-Simon Laplace counted about five thousand years of recorded

19
00:01:29.543 --> 00:01:35.867
sunrises. His count gave odds near two million to one for another sunrise.

20
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:40.730
Laplace added that an astronomer would give much higher odds.

21
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:44.118
The astronomer knew why days and seasons occur.

22
00:01:44.118 --> 00:01:47.867
The event stayed the same, but the evidence changed.

23
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:54.333
Every probability uses a group. Name that group, or the number has no clear meaning.

24
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:57.589
The group gives the number a base.

25
00:01:57.589 --> 00:02:02.667
The doctors still missed because they used the test result badly.

26
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:08.115
Bayes' rule shows how new evidence should change a probability.

27
00:02:08.115 --> 00:02:14.600
It begins with the base rate, which is how common the disease was before the test.

28
00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:19.541
Picture one thousand forty-year-old women at routine screening.

29
00:02:19.541 --> 00:02:24.867
Ten have breast cancer. That count gives the base rate: one in one hundred.

30
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:28.736
Eight of the ten women with cancer test positive.

31
00:02:28.736 --> 00:02:34.500
Ninety-five of the nine hundred and ninety women without cancer also test positive.

32
00:02:34.967 --> 00:02:40.775
In all, one hundred and three women get a positive result. Only eight have cancer.

33
00:02:40.775 --> 00:02:45.733
Eight out of one hundred and three is about seven point eight percent.

34
00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:52.079
Cancer is rare in this group, so most positive results come from women without

35
00:02:52.079 --> 00:02:57.567
cancer. The doctors focused on the test and lost sight of the base rate.

36
00:02:58.033 --> 00:03:01.412
Whole-number counts made the problem easier.

37
00:03:01.412 --> 00:03:07.861
In a 1995 study, correct answers rose from sixteen percent with percentages to about

38
00:03:07.861 --> 00:03:09.167
half with counts.

39
00:03:09.633 --> 00:03:15.571
Start with how common the event is. Then use the new evidence to update it.

40
00:03:15.571 --> 00:03:19.767
One test rarely turns a rare event into a common one.

41
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:26.967
Here, the group and test supported one useful number. We are not always that lucky.

42
00:03:27.433 --> 00:03:33.884
A coin that lands heads fifty times in one hundred tosses supports odds near one

43
00:03:33.884 --> 00:03:34.287
half.

44
00:03:34.287 --> 00:03:40.738
An unseen coin might also be assigned one half, but no toss supports that point.

45
00:03:40.738 --> 00:03:44.367
A range from zero to one shows our ignorance.

46
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:51.463
Daniel Ellsberg gave another example in 1961. An urn holds thirty red balls.

47
00:03:51.463 --> 00:03:57.133
The other sixty are black or yellow, but no one tells us the mix.

48
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.380
The chance of drawing red is exactly one third.

49
00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.367
The chance of black could be anywhere from zero to two thirds.

50
00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:10.067
A dot fits one answer; a range fits the other.

51
00:04:10.533 --> 00:04:17.567
Thin evidence calls for a range. One exact point would hide how little we know.

52
00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:21.672
A range shows the limits of today's evidence.

53
00:04:21.672 --> 00:04:26.033
A forecast adds a harder step: a claim about the future.

54
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.728
Forecasts use patterns from the past.

55
00:04:29.728 --> 00:04:36.534
In 1748, David Hume pointed out the hidden assumption: we expect the future to

56
00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:38.367
behave like the past.

57
00:04:38.833 --> 00:04:45.858
A forecast also uses a model, which is a simplified account of how something works.

58
00:04:45.858 --> 00:04:52.543
In 1963, Edward Lorenz showed that tiny changes at the start could lead to very

59
00:04:52.543 --> 00:04:54.067
different results.

60
00:04:54.533 --> 00:05:00.207
Europe's main weather center therefore runs its model fifty times with slightly

61
00:05:00.207 --> 00:05:05.666
different starting values. A tight group of runs supports a firmer forecast.

62
00:05:05.666 --> 00:05:08.467
A wide spread signals more uncertainty.

63
00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:15.735
Different future claims do different jobs. Forecasts start with current conditions.

64
00:05:15.735 --> 00:05:19.095
Projections depend on stated assumptions.

65
00:05:19.095 --> 00:05:23.767
Scenarios describe possible paths without assigning odds.

66
00:05:24.233 --> 00:05:27.543
Forecasting joins past evidence to a model.

67
00:05:27.543 --> 00:05:33.392
Name the kind of future claim, state its assumptions, and show the spread of

68
00:05:33.392 --> 00:05:34.700
possible results.

69
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.214
We now have a future claim with clear limits.

70
00:05:39.214 --> 00:05:42.867
The last step is to test many such claims.

71
00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:48.320
The Brier score measures the gap between a forecast and what happened.

72
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.030
Lower scores are better.

73
00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:55.800
A ninety-percent forecast earns a low cost when right and a high cost when wrong.

74
00:05:56.267 --> 00:06:00.780
One result may be luck. Many results reveal calibration.

75
00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:07.308
A calibrated set of seventy-percent forecasts comes true about seventy percent of

76
00:06:07.308 --> 00:06:08.033
the time.

77
00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.563
People are often too sure.

78
00:06:10.563 --> 00:06:16.752
In studies reviewed in 1974, ranges that people said were ninety-eight percent

79
00:06:16.752 --> 00:06:21.433
certain missed the answer about thirty percent of the time.

80
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:29.633
One outcome cannot test a forecast. A long record of scored forecasts can.

81
00:06:30.233 --> 00:06:33.407
Name the group before giving a probability.

82
00:06:33.407 --> 00:06:37.762
Start with the base rate, then update it with new evidence.

83
00:06:37.762 --> 00:06:40.271
Use a range when evidence is thin.

84
00:06:40.271 --> 00:06:44.036
For a future claim, name the model and assumptions.

85
00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:46.767
Test your work across many forecasts.

86
00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:49.284
Return to the positive test.

87
00:06:49.284 --> 00:06:54.974
The answer begins with one thousand similar women, then uses the test result to

88
00:06:54.974 --> 00:06:59.800
update the base rate. That path leads to seven point eight percent.

89
00:07:00.267 --> 00:07:02.781
Where does a probability come from?

90
00:07:02.781 --> 00:07:07.595
It comes from a named group and evidence that changes what we know.

91
00:07:07.595 --> 00:07:11.833
How do we test it? We score many forecasts, not one result.

92
00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:15.732
Even a well-built number can fail after we say it.

93
00:07:15.732 --> 00:07:21.033
The final video asks whether the listener heard the same probability we meant.
